00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:03.830 align:start position:0%
stratford-uneven district council is  currently consulting on a site  allocations plan  this short video explains policy sap 7  in the preferred options  site allocations plan which relates to  the identification of built up area  boundaries  basically boundaries have been drawn  around the existing extent of the  built-up areas  of towns and villages in the district  which are deemed large enough to be  classified as sustainable locations  for additional development over the  planned period  the purpose of a build superior boundary  is to distinguish between land inside a  settlement where new development is  acceptable in principle  from land outside of settlement where  development is generally not acceptable  except for some exemptions which relate  to appropriate development in the  countryside  these settlement boundaries therefore  help to prevent development encroaching  into the countryside  the boundaries include and exclude  certain types of land  the full list of land types can be found  annex 4 of the site allocations plan  but in effect land included with placed  dwellings and commercial properties and  their immediate curses  land excluded from a boundary relates to  land on the edge of a settlement that is  undeveloped  such as playing fields allotments  paddocks agricultural land  and other non-domestic and  non-commercial type uses  each boundary extends around the  periphery of the settlement  areas of open land located within the  settlements have not been removed from  the boundaries  they are located within the main body of  the settlement and as such  remain within the built-up area boundary  itself even though open land within the  boundary  may not be suitable for development due  to its immunity  recreation or other value  in some instances not every single  dwelling on the periphery of a  settlement will be included within the  boundary  for that settlement this is due to the  existence of non-domestic  and non-commercial land parcels lying  between the edge of a settlement and the  dwelling in question  however dwellings which sit outside a  boundary remain part of the settlement  and will maintain the same planning  rights of those properties located  within the boundary  i hope this has provided you with an  overview to our approach to identifying  built up area boundaries  throughout the district you can find out  more in the site allocations plan and  submit any comments you have by visiting  our website at www.stratford.gov  dot uk forward slash zappo
 

